--TEST--
`Core\Command::run` converts a `Core\Uuid` argument through the one parse `Core\Uuid::parse` reads, so an upper-case command line normalizes — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`, spec § 11
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The one *class* § 6 admits an argument at, matched nominally. Its grammar is
// `nvs_runtime::uuid`, which is also what `Core\Uuid::parse` and a
// `{id: Core\Uuid}` route capture read (`rule:routing/a-capture-narrows-to-a-closed-set`) — one home, so a
// command line's word, a program's parse and a request's segment cannot come
// to disagree about which spellings are a UUID.
//
// Two claims, and the second is why the case is worth a run: the value arrives
// as a `Core\Uuid` rather than as its text — `->toString()` is a member no
// `tainted string` has — and it arrives *normalized*, so the canonical form
// comes back lower-cased from a command line that shouted it.
class Fleet {
    #[Core\Command(name: "show", about: "Show one account")]
    public static function show(Core\Uuid $account): uint {
        echo "account: ", $account->toString(), "\n";
        return 0;
    }
}

uint $status = Core\Command::run();
echo "status: ", $status, "\n";
--ARGS--
show
F9168C5E-CEB2-4FAA-B6BF-329BF39FA1E4
--EXPECT--
account: f9168c5e-ceb2-4faa-b6bf-329bf39fa1e4
status: 0
